  "decision": "Two keywords, `succession` then `flow`, around the same FlowDeclaration and TypeBody that Flow takes. The pair is what distinguishes this production, and the two words are separate keywords rather than one token.",
  "notes": "THE SAME NAMING DEFECT AS Flow. The clause writes `SuccessionFlow = FeaturePrefix 'succession' 'flow' ItemFlowDeclaration TypeBody`, and ItemFlowDeclaration is defined nowhere while the clause's own FlowDeclaration is referenced nowhere. Checked, not assumed: ItemFlowDeclaration is absent from every derivation unit and from the Xtext production inventory. The pilot rule is `SuccessionFlow : FeaturePrefix 'succession' 'flow' FlowDeclaration TypeBody`. This rule uses FlowDeclaration, for the reasons set out at length on the Flow unit.\n\n`succession` AND `flow` ARE TWO TOKENS, NOT ONE. Both the clause and the Xtext write them as separate quoted literals, and both are independently in the pinned token set \u2014 `succession` opens a Succession and `flow` opens a Flow. Nothing lexical joins them.\n\nFOR A PARSER AUTHOR: this is the reason `succession` cannot be committed to on sight. Seeing `succession`, a parser must look at the next token to know whether it is parsing a Succession or a SuccessionFlow, and the corpus contains both \u2014 `succession first operated then inOperable;` and `succession flow exposure[1] of Exposure from step1.xrsl to step2.xsf;`. One token of lookahead past the keyword is enough, but zero is not.\n\nThe unbalanced parenthesis in this clause belongs to PayloadFeature, not to this production; see the Flow unit.\n\nCORPUS: `succession flow exposure[1] of Exposure from step1.xrsl to step2.xsf;` exercises both keywords, the `of` payload form, the `from`/`to` ends and the `;` body.",
